16632080078125, 0.02496337890625, 0.03863525390625, -0.0280609130859375, -0.0256805419921875, 0.006153106689453125, 0.00531768798828125, 0.005584716796875, 0.01316070556640625, 0.0007481575012207031, -0.00962066650390625, 0.007904052734375, -0.003292083740234375, -0.020172119140625, 0.031524658203125, 0.01384735107421875, 0.03497314453125, 0.0022792816162109375, 0.0179443359375, -0.01068878173828125, 0.0253753662109375, 0.0132293701171875, -0.0023517608642578125, 0.0142974853515625, -0.0254364013671875, -0.0009350776672363281, 0.00560760498046875, -0.005126953125, -0.034820556640625, 0.0277252197265625, 0.00820159912109375, 0.0258941650390625, -0.015716552734375, 0.01335906982421875, -0.012969970703125, 0.00878143310546875, 0.03582763671875, -0.01183319091796875, 0.0219268798828125, -0.0036468505859375, -0.0158843994140625, 0.0009784698486328125, -0.034515380859375, -0.028839111328125, 0.0174560546875, -0.0125885009765625, 0.03515625, -0.0031795501708984375, 0.0236358642578125, 0.023956298828125, 0.0007309913635253906, -0.01727294921875, -0.008758544921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose and Rationale:   \n\n**Background:** High-quality, structured radiology reporting is critical for clinical care, interpretation, and auditing. Despite widespread recognition, many reports still rely on non-standardized language, anatomical ambiguity, and incomplete documentation, undermining QA and workflow efficiency.  \n**Prior Limitations:** Existing solutions, such as strictly rule-based sectioning tools and single-agent large language models, have struggled to effectively process reports with complex, multi-organ findings and often lack transparency, adaptability, or scalability (see Supplementary Table 1). Furthermore, dependence on cloud infrastructure or third-party data sources can impede local adaptation and privacy compliance.  \n**System Innovation:** To address these limitations, we developed a multi-agent AI system that operates entirely on local infrastructure, combining deterministic anatomical parsing, a fine-tuned open-source language model, and interpretable QA logic. This hybrid architecture is designed to deliver robust free-text report structuring, surface clinically relevant inconsistencies, and ensure transparency and real-time adaptability to an institution\u2019s evolving needs.\n\nMaterials and Methods:  \n\n**Data Cohort:**  \nA retrospective exploratory study was conducted using 638 CT radiology reports (chest, abdomen, pelvis) dictated in 2023\u20132024 by 15 board-certified radiologists at a single academic center. Inclusion: adult patient age (>18 years), examination within study period, dictated by one of the study radiologists, and full availability of report text plus accompanying demographic metadata. Exclusion: incomplete, pediatric, or heavily redacted reports. Sampling was random based on these criteria.\n\n**AI System Architecture:**  \n\n1. **Rule-Based Section Labeling Agent**\n   - **Lexicon and Regular Expressions:** Lexicons of >50 anatomical entities were curated from ACR and RSNA vocabulary standards, then refined by iterative keyword extraction and manual correction from a representative sample (n=200) of in-house reports. Regular expressions prioritized precise anatomical terms (e.g., \u201cliver,\u201d \u201cadrenal,\u201d \u201ckidney\u201d) and excluded ambiguous synonyms. Each sentence was tokenized and matched against this hierarchy; sentences with multiple entities received the highest-priority label (e.g., if \u201cliver\u201d and \u201ckidney\u201d appear together, assign \u201cliver\u201d per clinical convention). Those with no match were assigned \u201cmiscellaneous.\u201d\n   - **Output:** Anatomical section labels for each sentence, facilitating downstream structuring.\n\n2. **Locally Deployed Language Model Agent**\n   - **Selection/Preprocessing:** Llama 2 13B was selected after benchmarking against smaller (7B, 3B) and cloud-based models for accuracy and resource requirements (see Supplementary Table 2). Training data involved 15,000 de-identified, non-overlapping CT report sections, preprocessed by removing named entities and mapping to canonical section labels. Reports with <2 sentences or >15% redacted text were excluded from training.\n   - **Fine-Tuning and Validation:** The model was fine-tuned using supervised learning with Adam optimizer (PyTorch v1.13, HuggingFace Transformers v4.32), a learning rate of 1e-5, batch size 12, over 4 epochs, and validated against an internal 500-section holdout set (see Figure S1). Section assignment thresholds required >80% lexical overlap with labeled ground truth to accept model predictions.\n   - **Output:** Structured, labeled reports mapped to predefined anatomical sections.\n\n3. **Deterministic QA Agent**\n   - **Operation:** Used explicit rules for error identification:\n     - **Section mismatch:** Flag if a clinically significant abnormality (e.g., \u201cmass,\u201d \u201cembolism\u201d) appears in Findings but not referenced or explained (including negated/uncertain) in Impression (NegEx-based negation pipeline).\n     - **Gender-anatomy conflict:** Compare presence of gender-specific organ terms with patient gender in structured metadata (e.g., \u201cuterus\u201d mentioned for male patient).\n     - **Undocumented communication:** Flag if terms like \u201cSTAT/urgent/critical\u201d occur without accompanying documentation of provider notification (requires \u201cnotified\u201d or equivalent string in relevant section).\n   - **Full rules:** Provided in Supplementary Table S2.\n   - **Output:** Binary QA flags for each of the three error categories.\n\n**Evaluation and Sampling:**  \n\nTo evaluate, a stratified review subset was created. Reports were first categorized by QA flag presence (as per system output):  \n- 80 with \u201csection mismatch\u201d,  \n- 5 with \u201cgender-anatomy conflict\u201d,  \n- 5 with \u201cundocumented communication\u201d,  \n- 553 unflagged.  \n\nFrom these, 45 were selected: 20 randomly drawn with section mismatch, 10 with gender-anatomy conflict, 10 with undocumented communication, and 5 unflagged controls (to ensure adequate common/rare error representation and manageable reviewer burden). Each report was represented only once by its primary error flag. Figure 1 summarizes the flow of sampling and case selection.\n\n**Reviewer Scoring and Metrics:**  \n\nTwo board-certified abdominal radiologists (each >5 years\u2019 experience) independently reviewed cases using a rubric (see Table 1) with four metrics:\n- **Fidelity:** Degree of accurate structure preservation.\n- **Accuracy:** Correctness of label assignment.\n- **Omission:** Whether required findings/abnormalities were missing from structured output.\n- **Fabrication:** Introduction of artifacts/details absent from original report.  \n\nEach metric scored as \u201cexcellent,\u201d \u201cgood,\u201d or \u201cfair,\u201d further defined in Table 1. Discrepancies were resolved by discussion, or, if unresolved, by a senior radiologist adjudicator. Reviewers were blinded to error category, metadata, and each other\u2019s ratings.\n\n**Statistical Analysis:**  \n\nAnalyses were descriptive to avoid inferential bias due to the non-random, stratified design. Proportions were reported with 95% Wilson confidence intervals (scipy.stats v1.10.1; see supplement for code). Reliability was evaluated via Cohen\u2019s kappa (sklearn v1.2.2) for binary (\u201ccorrect/incorrect\u201d) and weighted for three-level ratings.  \n- Descriptive statistics were chosen because stratified oversampling (spectrum bias) artificially inflates error prevalence and estimates are not generalizable.\n- No hypothesis testing or p-values were calculated.\n\nResults:  \n\n**Overall Output:**  \nThe system restructured all 638 reports (22,270 sentences). The QA agent flagged:\n- 80/638 (12.5%) for section mismatch,\n- 5/638 (0.8%) for gender-anatomy conflict,\n- 5/638 (0.8%) for undocumented communication.  \n\nExamples (Table 2):  \n- *Section mismatch:* \u201cRenal mass\u201d described in Findings, not addressed in Impression.\n- *Gender-anatomy conflict:* \u201cProstate\u201d present in a female patient report.\n- *Undocumented communication:* \u201cPulmonary embolism\u201d stated, no provider notification documented.\n\n**Stratified Review Results (n=45, see Table 3):**\n- 31/45 (69%, 95% CI: 54\u201381%) excellent/good structuring,\n- 2/45 (4%, 95% CI: 0\u201312%) incorrect (due to compound findings misassigned by hierarchy),\n- 12/45 (27%, 95% CI: 15\u201341%) required consensus resolution (mainly \u201cgood\u201d vs \u201cfair\u201d on label boundary),\n- Cohen\u2019s kappa for correct/incorrect: 0.49; weighted kappa: 0.55,\n- 40/45 reached consensus after discussion, 5 by senior adjudicator,\n- 0/45 omission or fabrication events (upper 95% CI < 7%).\n\nQuality grading distribution: \u201cexcellent\u201d 17/45 (38%), \u201cgood\u201d 21/45 (46%), \u201cfair\u201d 7/45 (16%). Most persistent errors were due to limitations in parsing multi-organ/compound findings (see Supplementary Table S3), where deterministic hierarchy forced single-label assignment.\n\n**Standalone Comparison:** No commercial/open-source QA tool for identical workflow exists, but a pilot manual-only sectioning of 20 cases had similar \u201cexcellent/good\u201d rates (90% vs 84%), supporting system validity.\n\n**Transparency and Interpretability Evidence:**  \nThe multi-agent approach allowed direct tracing of errors to specific pipeline stages. During review, flagged errors could be independently corroborated against corresponding section or rule (e.g., anatomical mislabeling by parser, over-flagging by QA module), supporting transparency and error traceability (see examples, Table 2).\n\nDiscussion:  \n\n**Limitations (Grouped and Linked):**  \n1. **Single-institution & Custom Lexicon:** The use of single-center, single-language data and institution-specific conventions may reduce generalizability and introduce linguistic bias. Cross-institution validation is needed, as some errors (see Supplement Table S5) emerged only when external phraseologies were tested.\n2. **Sampling and Statistical Inference:** Stratified sampling was used to ensure diverse error coverage, but oversampling of rare errors (spectrum bias) inflates reported rates, and descriptive statistics were appropriately prioritized over population inference.\n3. **Reviewer Bias:** Familiarity with in-house report style may have increased reviewer-graded accuracy; strict blinding and adjudication protocols were used, but results may differ with external or multi-institutional annotators.\n4. **Lexicon/Hierarchy Coverage:** The rule-based anatomical hierarchy cannot capture all compound/multi-organ statements; this was the chief source of persistent structuring errors visible in error analysis tables. Additional adversarial and cross-style testing will be essential for future improvements.\n5. **Reproducibility and Transparency:** All core lexicons, rules, and annotation protocols are provided in the supplement; Llama 2 fine-tuning details and software versions are explicitly reported for reproducibility.\n\n**Strengths and Distinctives:**  \n- **Transparency and Interpretability"}}